    "text": "A bile is a sacred tree. It often grows beside a holy well or another honored place. People in Ireland still hang strips of cloth on the branches. These cloth strips are called clooties. The tree droops with these offerings today.\n\nIn old times a bile marked the place where a king was installed. Its branches were cut to shape the ruler's staff. A god named Bile also appears in Irish legend. He is the ancestral father of the Milesians. They were the last invaders of Ireland.\n\nIt may be that the tree holds a link to the god. The link is not certain. The term bile names a sacred tree of any species. Oak, yew, and ash held the chief power. The Dindsenchas, the place poems, sing of the great trees of Ireland. They tell of an oak that bore nuts and apples along with its acorns. The Yew of Ross was a firm strong god. The Ash of Uisneach fell and spread out fifty miles.",
